{"qid":"Q2649311","names":["Sultanate of Perak"],"title":"Sultanate of Perak","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Sultanate of Perak was founded c. 1528–1529 when Muzaffar Shah, a prince of the fallen Malacca Sultanate, was invited to rule the tin-rich Perak river valley on the Malay Peninsula; control of its extensive tin deposits made Perak the most strategically contested Malay state, drawing Dutch, British, and Bugis competition across two centuries. Internal succession disputes in the 1860s–70s among rival claimants and Chinese tin-mining factions destabilized the state, prompting British intervention; the Pangkor Treaty of 1874 formalized British protection and installed a Resident, but Sultan Abdullah's implication in the murder of the first Resident J.W.W. Birch in 1875 triggered the Perak War. Britain confirmed control and Perak was incorporated into the Federated Malay States in 1896.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Perak's core territory along the Perak River is well-documented in British colonial records from the 1870s onward; earlier borders with Selangor and interior polities are less precisely attested.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Sultan of Perak","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultan_of_Perak"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Pangkor Treaty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pangkor_Treaty"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Sultan of Perak","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Muzaffar Shah I","nativeName":"سلطان مظفر شاه","roman":"Muzaffar Shah I","start":1528,"end":1549,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Mansur Shah I","nativeName":